<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">My Country, 'Tis of Thee</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">written and first sung <a href="/years/1831">1831</a>; published <a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;My Country, 'Tis of Thee&quot;, also called &quot;America&quot;, was written in 1831 by Samuel Francis Smith while he was a student at the Andover Theological Seminary in Andover, Massachusetts. The composer Lowell Mason had asked him to translate, or write new words for, a collection of German songs, one of which was set to the melody of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot;, the de facto national anthem of the United Kingdom.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Outside the empire the melody served Prussia (1795–1918), the German Emperor (1871–1918), the Russian Empire (1816–1833) and Switzerland (until 1961), and it is still used for Liechtenstein's national anthem, Norway's royal anthem and the American song &quot;<b>My Country, 'Tis of Thee</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>My Country, 'Tis of Thee</b>&quot;, also called &quot;America&quot;, was written in 1831 by Samuel Francis Smith while he was a student at the Andover Theological Seminary in Andover, Massachusetts.</blockquote><a href="/songs/my-country-tis-of-thee">My Country, 'Tis of Thee</a></li><li><blockquote>Before 3 March 1931, when &quot;The Star-Spangled Banner&quot; became the official national anthem, it served with &quot;Hail, Columbia&quot; and &quot;<b>My Country, 'Tis of Thee</b>&quot; as one of the country's unofficial anthems.</blockquote><a href="/songs/yankee-doodle">Yankee Doodle</a></li></ul></section>
